  "brief": "The second mock round, on a different shape. Three routers in a line, a LAN at each end, and the server hanging off the middle one. Nothing on the routers is configured. PC1 has been addressed already — check it anyway.\n\n  LAN 1       10.10.1.0/24     R1 G0/0 = .1      PC1 .10 (already set)\n  WAN R1–R2   10.10.100.0/30   R1 G0/1 = .1      R2 G0/0/0 = .2\n  Server LAN  10.10.2.0/24     R2 G0/0/2 = .1    SRV .10 (already set)\n  WAN R2–R3   10.10.200.0/30   R2 G0/0/1 = .1    R3 G0/0 = .2\n  LAN 3       10.10.3.0/24     R3 G0/1 = .1      PC3 via DHCP from R3\n\nR1 and R3 are stubs — one default route each. R2 is in the middle and sees both ends, so it needs a specific route in each direction. R2 is a 4331; its ports are numbered 0/0/0 through 0/0/2.",
  "watchFor": "R2 needs two routes, not one. A default route on R2 has nowhere sensible to point — it is the middle of the network, not the edge. Forgetting the second one gives you a network where one end works and the other silently does not.",
  "topologyRef": "branch",
  "clearConfig": [
    "R1",
    "R2",
    "R3",
    "PC3"
  ],
  "faults": [
    {
      "type": "wrong-mask",
      "device": "PC1",
      "iface": "FastEthernet0",
      "mask": "255.255.0.0"
    }
  ],
  "tasks": [
    {
      "id": "r1",
      "text": "Address both of R1's interfaces and bring them up.",
      "checks": [
        {
          "type": "interface",
          "device": "R1",
          "if": "GigabitEthernet0/0",
          "ip": "10.10.1.1",
          "cidr": 24,
          "up": true
        },
        {
          "type": "interface",
          "device": "R1",
          "if": "GigabitEthernet0/1",
          "ip": "10.10.100.1",
          "cidr": 30,
          "up": true
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "r2",
      "text": "Address all three of R2's interfaces and bring them up.",
      "hint": "g0/0/0 to R1, g0/0/1 to R3, g0/0/2 to the server LAN.",
      "checks": [
        {
          "type": "interface",
          "device": "R2",
          "if": "GigabitEthernet0/0/0",
          "ip": "10.10.100.2",
          "cidr": 30,
          "up": true
        },
        {
          "type": "interface",
          "device": "R2",
          "if": "GigabitEthernet0/0/1",
          "ip": "10.10.200.1",
          "cidr": 30,
          "up": true
        },
        {
          "type": "interface",
          "device": "R2",
          "if": "GigabitEthernet0/0/2",
          "ip": "10.10.2.1",
          "cidr": 24,
          "up": true
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "r3",
      "text": "Address both of R3's interfaces and bring them up.",
      "checks": [
        {
          "type": "interface",
          "device": "R3",
          "if": "GigabitEthernet0/0",
          "ip": "10.10.200.2",
          "cidr": 30,
          "up": true
        },
        {
          "type": "interface",
          "device": "R3",
          "if": "GigabitEthernet0/1",
          "ip": "10.10.3.1",
          "cidr": 24,
          "up": true
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "r1-default",
      "text": "Give R1 a default route toward R2.",
      "check": {
        "type": "defaultRoute",
        "device": "R1",
        "nextHop": "10.10.100.2"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "r3-default",
      "text": "Give R3 a default route toward R2.",
      "check": {
        "type": "defaultRoute",
        "device": "R3",
        "nextHop": "10.10.200.1"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "r2-west",
      "text": "On R2, add the route to LAN 1.",
      "hint": "ip route 10.10.1.0 255.255.255.0 10.10.100.1",
      "check": {
        "type": "route",
        "device": "R2",
        "prefix": "10.10.1.0",
        "cidr": 24,
        "nextHop": "10.10.100.1"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "r2-east",
      "text": "On R2, add the route to LAN 3.",
      "hint": "This is the one people forget. ip route 10.10.3.0 255.255.255.0 10.10.200.2",
      "check": {
        "type": "route",
        "device": "R2",
        "prefix": "10.10.3.0",
        "cidr": 24,
        "nextHop": "10.10.200.2"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "pc1-mask",
      "text": "PC1 was addressed before you got here, and it is wrong. Fix the mask.",
      "hint": "ipconfig on PC1. A /16 makes it treat all of 10.10.0.0 as local, so it never uses its gateway.",
      "check": {
        "type": "host",
        "device": "PC1",
        "ip": "10.10.1.10",
        "cidr": 24,
        "gateway": "10.10.1.1"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "dhcp",
      "text": "On R3, build a DHCP pool named LAN3 for 10.10.3.0/24, excluding .1 through .9, handing out the gateway and DNS at 10.10.2.10.",
      "checks": [
        {
          "type": "dhcpExcluded",
          "device": "R3",
          "start": "10.10.3.1",
          "end": "10.10.3.9"
        },
        {
          "type": "dhcpPool",
          "device": "R3",
          "name": "LAN3",
          "network": "10.10.3.0",
          "defaultRouter": "10.10.3.1",
          "dnsServer": "10.10.2.10"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "pc3-lease",
      "text": "Put PC3 on DHCP and confirm the lease.",
      "check": {
        "type": "dhcpLease",
        "device": "PC3",
        "inNetwork": "10.10.3.0",
        "gateway": "10.10.3.1"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "reach-13",
      "text": "Prove PC1 can reach PC3 — end to end, across both routers.",
      "check": {
        "type": "reachability",
        "from": "PC1",
        "to": "PC3"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "reach-1s",
      "text": "Prove PC1 can reach the server.",
      "check": {
